<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             x:Class="ConditioningControlPanel.Avalonia.Views.Tabs.SpiralTabView"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             d:DesignHeight="620" d:DesignWidth="1460">

    <!-- ================================================================
         THE SPIRAL ROOM (CONTRACT-FUSE-0816 2.4) - three states in one tab.
         Ported from ConditioningControlPanel/Views/Tabs/SpiralTabView.xaml.

           FOG      - a ceremony is owed or scheduled. A full-bleed fog layer
                      (SpiralFirstLightVisual held before its first stroke, with
                      its ambient clock free-running) under centred copy in the
                      fuse's voice. Nothing is clickable.
           FIRST    - the one-shot reveal, played INSIDE this tab the moment the
           LIGHT      user's own ceremony commits. Same canvas, clock running.
           SPIRAL   - the /embed/spiral canvas at ?mode=map, full tab width,
                      behind a loading splash until it has actually navigated.
                      Falls back to the waiting panel, which is today's live path
                      because the map route is not deployed yet.

         THE AIRSPACE STRATEGY, and it is why this file is shaped like this.
         A native web view is a child surface: it paints OVER every framework
         element in the same window whatever the z-order says. So the embed is not
         merely hidden while the fog or the reveal is up - it is NEVER CONSTRUCTED.
         The code-behind builds it on entering the spiral state and drops it on the
         way out, so there is no idle browser behind a tab nobody is looking at and
         nothing can paint over the fog.

         The SPLASH is the same problem one layer down. A constructed browser paints
         its own dark slab the instant its surface exists, which is what made the
         wait read as a hang. So EmbedHost is parked invisible while the engine
         loads and is only promoted once the embed has something to show. Until then
         the splash owns the surface and nothing native can paint over it. On a
         machine with NO engine there is nothing to hide - WebHost draws a legible
         fallback panel of its own - so the splash is skipped entirely.

         Everything visible here is authored in code-behind or literal: the copy is
         hardcoded English by contract (4, see DescentFuseCopy) and every colour is
         the fuse's own gold, never the mod accent.

         ANIMATION LAW: opacity and transform only, everywhere in this file. The two
         BlurEffects below are STATIC - set once, never animated - and the glow they
         sit on breathes by opacity.
         ================================================================ -->

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!-- ponytail: local copy of ConditioningControlPanel/App.xaml:Font.Mono, hoist when a second view needs it -->
        <FontFamily x:Key="Font.Mono">Cascadia Mono, Consolas, Courier New</FontFamily>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <Grid Background="#0A0514">

        <!-- The fog / reveal canvas.
             ponytail: needs Controls/SpiralFirstLightVisual (a WPF DrawingVisual), wired when the
             reveal is redrawn for this head. The WPF code-behind builds it with an accent Color
             and drops it in here; this host stays empty, so the fog era is its copy on the bare
             ground - which is what a machine that cannot run the canvas would show anyway. -->
        <Grid x:Name="FogHost"/>

        <!-- The fog era's embers: a handful of gold specks drifting up through the
             weather, seeded and driven from code-behind. Clipped, hit-test dead,
             and it never exists at all under reduced motion. -->
        <Canvas x:Name="EmberHost" IsVisible="False"
                IsHitTestVisible="False" ClipToBounds="True"/>

        <!-- The fog era's copy. Hit-test invisible as a whole: there is nothing to
             click in the fog, and a caret or a hover state would imply otherwise. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="FogCopy" IsVisible="False"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    MaxWidth="620" IsHitTestVisible="False">

            <TextBlock x:Name="FogEyebrow" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       FontSize="12" Foreground="#E0B052" Opacity="0.85"
                       FontFeatures="smcp"/>

            <TextBlock x:Name="FogLine" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="0,14,0,0" FontSize="21" LineHeight="30"
                       TextAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Foreground="#F2ECF7"/>

            <!-- THE HERO. Two TextBlocks carrying the same string: a blurred gold
                 duplicate underneath for the bloom, the crisp digits over it. The
                 glow's Text and FontSize follow the crisp one so the readout can
                 change size (digits are big, the "any moment now" phrase is not)
                 without either half being retyped twice.

                 The pulse and the phase flare both live on this host: the scale is
                 the heartbeat, the glow's opacity is the flare.

                 No RenderTransformOrigin and no declared ScaleTransform: Avalonia's
                 default origin IS the centre (WPF's is the top-left corner, which is
                 why the original had to say so), and the pulse animates the whole
                 RenderTransform as a TransformOperations value - see StartFogFx. -->
            <Grid x:Name="FogDigitsHost" Margin="0,30,0,0"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center">

                <TextBlock x:Name="FogDigitsGlow" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="{Binding #FogDigits.Text}"
                           FontSize="{Binding #FogDigits.FontSize}"
                           FontFamily="{DynamicResource Font.Mono}"
                           FontWeight="Bold" Foreground="#E0B052" Opacity="0.42"
                           FontFeatures="tnum"
                           IsHitTestVisible="False">
                    <TextBlock.Effect>
                        <BlurEffect Radius="16"/>
                    </TextBlock.Effect>
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock x:Name="FogDigits" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           FontSize="56" FontWeight="Bold"
                           FontFamily="{DynamicResource Font.Mono}"
                           Foreground="#E0B052"
                           FontFeatures="tnum"/>
            </Grid>

            <!-- The ornament: one gold hairline that breathes under the digits. A
                 Rectangle rather than a Border so nothing about it is layout. -->
            <Rectangle x:Name="FogHairline" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="0,20,0,0" Width="190" Height="1"
                       Fill="#E0B052" Opacity="0.30"/>

            <TextBlock x:Name="FogTail" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="0,22,0,0" FontSize="12" LineHeight="19"
                       TextAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Foreground="#8AC9C4D6"/>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The embed's slab. Empty until the spiral state builds one, and invisible
             rather than shown until that embed has something to paint - see the
             airspace note at the top of this file.

             THE 32px TOP INSET IS THE HELP CHIP'S AIRSPACE. A native web view paints
             over every framework element in the window whatever the z-order says, so
             a "?" drawn on top of a full-bleed embed would simply stop existing the
             moment the canvas revealed. Reserving a strip the browser is never
             arranged into is the only way the affordance survives up here, and it
             costs the map 32px it was not using. The fog and the reveal keep their
             full bleed: they are the two states with no browser in them, and the chip
             is not shown in either. -->
        <Grid x:Name="EmbedHost" IsVisible="False" Margin="0,32,0,0"/>

        <!-- THE SPLASH. Opaque, because its whole job is to be the thing on screen
             while a browser is being built behind it. Drawn from a spiral the
             code-behind builds as geometry; the ring, the arm and the three dots
             are all opacity and rotation. -->
        <Grid x:Name="SpiralSplash" IsVisible="False" Background="#0A0514">
            <StackPanel HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">

                <Grid Width="104" Height="104" HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                    <Ellipse x:Name="SplashHalo" Width="104" Height="104"
                             Stroke="#E0B052" StrokeThickness="1" Opacity="0.16"/>

                    <!-- WPF's StrokeStartLineCap + StrokeEndLineCap are one property here. -->
                    <Path x:Name="SplashGlyph" Width="78" Height="78"
                          Stretch="Uniform" Stroke="#E0B052" StrokeThickness="2.2"
                          StrokeLineCap="Round" Opacity="0.92"/>
                </Grid>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            Margin="0,26,0,0">
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SplashLine" FontSize="14"
                               Foreground="#E0B052" Opacity="0.88"/>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SplashDot1" FontSize="14" Foreground="#E0B052"
                               Opacity="0.15" Text="."/>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SplashDot2" FontSize="14" Foreground="#E0B052"
                               Opacity="0.15" Text="."/>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SplashDot3" FontSize="14" Foreground="#E0B052"
                               Opacity="0.15" Text="."/>
                </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- The waiting room. Same chrome as the fuse's corner readout, so a user
             who has seen the countdown recognises the voice. It gets its own
             ambient loop - a breathing line over a few drifting motes - because a
             static panel that says "finding you" reads as a panel that has
             stopped looking. -->
        <Border x:Name="WaitingPanel" IsVisible="False"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Padding="26,18" CornerRadius="10"
                Background="#B00A0514" BorderBrush="#40E0B052" BorderThickness="1">
            <StackPanel>
                <Grid HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock x:Name="WaitingGlow" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                               Text="{Binding #WaitingLine.Text}"
                               FontSize="16" Foreground="#E0B052" Opacity="0.5"
                               IsHitTestVisible="False">
                        <TextBlock.Effect>
                            <BlurEffect Radius="9"/>
                        </TextBlock.Effect>
                    </TextBlock>

                    <TextBlock x:Name="WaitingLine" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                               FontSize="16" Foreground="#E0B052"/>
                </Grid>

                <Canvas x:Name="WaitingMotes" Height="34" Width="216"
                        Margin="0,10,0,0" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        IsHitTestVisible="False" ClipToBounds="True"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- THE "?" - what the Spiral actually is, for the people who walked in here
             and found a map with no legend. Its CONTENT is the app's ordinary help
             popover (Controls.HelpPopover, attached in code-behind), so it behaves
             exactly like every other "?" in the app.

             ITS SKIN IS NOT THE APP'S, AND THAT IS THE FILE'S LAW, NOT A PREFERENCE.
             HelpButtonStyle is an 18px circle that hovers to the theme's hot accent,
             which is the mod accent, which this room may never wear (see the header,
             and SpiralRoomTests.TheFogEraWearsTheFusesOwnGoldAndNeverTheModAccent,
             which pins it by refusing this file ANY resource lookup that is not a
             font). So the chip is templated here in the fuse's own literal gold. Same
             shape, same size, same cursor, same meaning; the room's colour.

             DECLARED LAST so it is topmost over the splash and the waiting panel, and
             it lives in the 32px EmbedHost reserves at the top so the browser cannot
             paint it away. Hidden by default: the code-behind shows it only in the
             two states that are actually a room you are being asked to understand,
             and never over the fog or the reveal, where there is nothing to click by
             design.

             WPF's ControlTemplate.Triggers becomes a Style selector into the template:
             same two setters, same trigger. No ToolTip on purpose - the popover and a
             ToolTip must never both render off the same button. -->
        <Button x:Name="BtnSpiralHelp" IsVisible="False"
                HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="0,8,14,0" Width="18" Height="18" Padding="0"
                Background="Transparent" Cursor="Help">
            <Button.Styles>
                <Style Selector="Button:pointerover /template/ Border#chip">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="#22E0B052"/>
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#99E0B052"/>
                </Style>
            </Button.Styles>
            <Button.Template>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="chip" Width="18" Height="18" CornerRadius="9"
                            Background="#00E0B052" BorderBrush="#4DE0B052" BorderThickness="1">
                        <TextBlock Text="?" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Foreground="#B8E0B052" Margin="0,-1,0,0"
                                   HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Button.Template>
        </Button>

    </Grid>
</UserControl>
